# RL-02. Two identical coils — 100 mH with 100 Ω of copper — on 12 V, each switched by an IRFZ44N from the same gate
# drive. Both carry 120 mA when the gate falls at 5 ms. The bare coil has nowhere to send its current: it drives its
# drain up until the transistor breaks down near 55 V and burns the coil's energy itself. The other coil has a
# resistor path across it through SW1: its current turns into that loop, the drain rises only to 12 V + 120 mA · 100 Ω
# = 24 V, and the current dies along τ = L / (R + R_path). Open SW1 while it runs and the second coil spikes too.
circuit:rl-switch-off("Switching off an inductor") {
    meta {
        summary: "Two coils carrying 120 mA are switched off: the bare one kicks its drain to the transistor's 55 V breakdown, the one with a resistor path only to 24 V."
        description: """
            Idea: an inductor keeps its current flowing — the energy ½ L i² is in its magnetic field and has to go
            somewhere. Open its path and it raises whatever voltage it takes to push the current on: across a
            transistor that has just turned off, that is far more than the supply, until something breaks down.
            Give the current another path and the voltage is only what that path needs.

            Try: SW1 connects a 100 Ω path across the right-hand coil. Open it while the circuit runs: the right drain
            kicks as high as the left one. Close it and raise L: the spike lasts longer, but with the path it is no
            taller — its height is the current times the path's resistance. Lower the path's resistance and the
            spike shrinks, but the current takes longer to die.

            Watch: CH1, the gate drive, falling at 5 ms; CH2, the bare coil's drain — a spike to the breakdown,
            near 55 V, almost five times the supply; CH3, the coil with a path — a step to 24 V that decays along
            τ = L / (R + R_path) = 0.5 ms.

            Ask: where does the energy go in each case? Why does a smaller path resistance give a lower spike but a
            slower decay? What would a diode in place of the resistor do?
            """
        category: rl-circuits
        tags: rl, example, rl-02
        authors: "jMouse"
        license: "CERN-OHL-W-2.0"
    }
    parameter:L(100 mH, in: 10 mH .. 1 H, step: 10 mH, "L, each coil")
    parameter:R_path(100 Ω, in: 10 Ω .. 1 kΩ, step: 10 Ω, "The resistor path across the right-hand coil")
    parameter:f(100 Hz, in: 10 Hz .. 1 kHz, step: 10 Hz, "Gate drive frequency")
    part:V1(voltage-source, [positive: vcc, negative: 0], U_output: 12 V)
    part:G1(square-generator, [positive: drive, negative: 0], high: 10 V, low: 0 V, frequency: f, duty: 0.5)
    part:RA(resistor, [vcc, bare_coil], resistance: 100 Ω)
    part:LA(inductor, [bare_coil, bare], inductance: L)
    part:QA(irfz44n, [gate: drive, drain: bare, source: 0])
    part:RB(resistor, [vcc, tamed_coil], resistance: 100 Ω)
    part:LB(inductor, [tamed_coil, tamed], inductance: L)
    part:QB(irfz44n, [gate: drive, drain: tamed, source: 0])
    part:SW1(switch, [a: vcc, b: path], closed: 1)
    part:RP(resistor, [path, tamed], resistance: R_path)
    place:V1(at: 240 600, rotate: 90)
    place:G1(at: 520 920, rotate: 90)
    place:RA(at: 880 320, rotate: 90)
    place:LA(at: 880 520, rotate: 90)
    place:QA(at: 840 760)
    place:RB(at: 1320 320, rotate: 90)
    place:LB(at: 1320 520, rotate: 90)
    place:QB(at: 1280 760)
    place:SW1(at: 1560 320, rotate: 90)
    place:RP(at: 1560 520, rotate: 90)
    mark:Ja(junction, at: 880 160)
    mark:Jt(junction, at: 1320 160)
    mark:Jd(junction, at: 1320 640)
    mark:Jg(junction, at: 640 760)
    mark:Jb1(junction, at: 240 1120)
    mark:Jb2(junction, at: 520 1120)
    mark:Jb3(junction, at: 880 1120)
    mark:Jb4(junction, at: 1320 1120)
    mark:GND1(ground, at: 520 1200)
    wire:W1(V1.positive, to: Ja.j, via: 240 160)
    wire:W2(Ja.j, to: RA.positive)
    wire:W3(RA.negative, to: LA.positive)
    wire:W4(LA.negative, to: QA.drain)
    wire:W5(Ja.j, to: Jt.j)
    wire:W6(Jt.j, to: RB.positive)
    wire:W7(RB.negative, to: LB.positive)
    wire:W8(LB.negative, to: Jd.j)
    wire:W9(Jd.j, to: QB.drain)
    wire:W10(Jt.j, to: SW1.a, via: 1560 160)
    wire:W11(SW1.b, to: RP.positive)
    wire:W12(RP.negative, to: Jd.j, via: 1560 640)
    wire:W13(G1.positive, to: Jg.j, via: 520 760)
    wire:W14(Jg.j, to: QA.gate)
    wire:W15(Jg.j, to: QB.gate, via: [640 960, 1080 960, 1080 760])
    wire:W16(V1.negative, to: Jb1.j)
    wire:W17(Jb1.j, to: Jb2.j)
    wire:W18(G1.negative, to: Jb2.j)
    wire:W19(Jb2.j, to: Jb3.j)
    wire:W20(QA.source, to: Jb3.j)
    wire:W21(Jb3.j, to: Jb4.j)
    wire:W22(QB.source, to: Jb4.j)
    wire:W23(Jb2.j, to: GND1.ground)
    probes {
        probe:CH1(drive, 5 V/div)
        probe:CH2(bare, 10 V/div)
        probe:CH3(tamed, 10 V/div)
        time(1 ms/div)
    }
    expect("both coils carry 120 mA when they are switched off", from: "after 5 τ: 12 V / 100 Ω · (1 − e⁻⁵), τ = L / R = 1 ms") {
        measure(maximum, current: RA)
        within(115 mA .. 121 mA)
    }
    expect("the bare coil kicks its drain up to the transistor's breakdown", from: "nowhere else for 120 mA to go: the IRFZ44N's V(BR)DSS, 55 V") {
        measure(maximum, bare)
        within(50 V .. 75 V)
    }
    expect("the coil with a path lifts its drain only to 24 V", from: "V = 12 V + 120 mA · 100 Ω, the current turned into the path") {
        measure(maximum, tamed)
        near(24 V, +- 4%)
    }
    analysis(transient, for: 8 ms)
    view:transient_response(primary)
}
